    <md:content-id>m02129</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.3.3: Additional Resources</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b41dd914-10eb-4cf8-8df3-37562b4e2ef7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x - h)^2 = k\)</title>
<para id="para-00001">We can use the square root property to solve an equation of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\) as well.</para>
<para id="para-00002">Notice that the quadratic term, \(x\), in the original form \(ax^2=k\) is replaced with \((x−h)\).</para>
<para id="para-00003">\(a {\style{color:red}x}^{2}=k \quad a{\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}x}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}h}{\style{color:red})}^{2}=k\)</para>
<para id="para-00004">Like before, the first step is to isolate the term that has the variable squared. In this case, a binomial is being squared.</para>
<para id="para-00005">Once the binomial is isolated, by dividing each side by the coefficient of \(a\), the square root property can be used on \((x−h)^2\).</para>
<para id="para-00006">Solve \(4(y−7)^2=48\).</para>
<para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Isolate the quadratic term and make its coefficient 1.</para>
<para id="para-00008"> So, divide both sides of the equation by 4.</para>
<para id="para-00009">\(4(y−7)^2=48\)
  \((y−7)^2=12\)</para>
<para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the square root property on the binomial.</para>
<para id="para-00011">  \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)</para>
<para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify the radical by pulling out the factors that are perfect squares.</para>
<para id="para-00013">  Solve for \(y\). Rewrite the two solutions.</para>
<para id="para-00014">\(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)
  \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{(4 \cdot 3})\)
  \(y−7= \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\)
  \(y =7 \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\)
  \(y =7+2 \sqrt{3}\), \(y=7−2 \sqrt{3}\) </para>
<para id="para-00015"><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>
  Check the solutions.</para>
<para id="para-00016">\(\begin{array}{rrr} 4(y-7)^{2}=48 &amp; 4(y-7)^{2}=48 \\ 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}+}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(-2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} &amp; 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} \end{array} \)</para>
<note class="try"><title>Try It: Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x-h)^2=k\)</title>
<para id="para-00017">Solve the following quadratic equations.</para>
<example id="exam-00001"><exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00018">\(3(a−3)^2=363\) </para>
    <para id="para-00019"> \(\frac{1}{2} (b+2)^2=200\) </para>
    <para id="para-00020">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00021">Here is how to solve these quadratic equations of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\):</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
    <item> \(3(a−3)^2 =363\) 
      \((a−3)^2=121\)
      \(a−3= \pm 11\)
      \(a−3=11\)      \(a−3=-11\)
      \(a= 14\) and \(a=-8\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
    <item> \(\frac{1}{2}(b+2)^2=200\)
      \((b+2)^2=400\)
      \(b+2= \pm 20\)
      \(b+2=20\)      \(b+2=-20\)
      \(b=18\) and \(b=-22\)</item>
  </list>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
